        title       The suite's own Spec, Conformance.spec
        status      done
        priority    high
        description
            Write `packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/Conformance.spec` with skill:to-spec, next to
            the suite (README item 7):

            - the Port exposes no runtime internals and any runtime implementing it gets the same
              verdicts;
            - expected results come from a cited Spec rule, never from running the runtime;
            - every check has a mutant it kills;
            - the null runtime fails every case;
            - runs are deterministic from a seed;
            - the verdict format and the old-suite comparison.

            The README keeps the how-to.

            - **May change:** the new Spec, `workflow/spec.index`, the README.
            - **Evidence:** this Task's comments.
            - **False pass:** a Spec restating the code's current behaviour → caught by
              skill:check-spec by an agent that did not write it.
            - **Gates:** `workflow check`.
            - **User's read:** the Spec's guarantees.
        acceptance
            The Spec exists, `workflow check` passes, and an independent skill:check-spec finds
            no gap in its contract.
        comment     2026-11-09T17:03:59Z
            **Result:** @spec.Conformance (`workflow/specs/conformance/Conformance.spec`, status proposed) is written
            with skill:to-spec and has no contract gap left after five independent skill:check-spec rounds (Opus lead,
            4 Haiku sidecars each; commits `4420d200`, `5959f541`, `5b627068`, `a87b896e`, `0df8e2a9`) and one
            convergence redesign. Design review: not applicable. Final size: R1 to R14, I1 to I44, T1 to T28, Q1 to
            Q14 (first draft: 11 Requirements, 30 Invariants, 23 tests, 7 open questions). Gates: `workflow format
            --write`, `workflow check` valid, `workflow refs --dangling` none, no line over 120 columns.

            **What it guarantees** (derived from the suite's purpose and the README's decisions, not from today's
            code):

            - R1 to R3: the Port carries public behaviour only (data values are JSON or Schema-encoded JSON; one
              settling exception, `pending`); verdicts never depend on which runtime runs; adapters translate and never
              fill reports in from suite facts. R2's stated limits are bounded: prototype adapter only, naming cases,
              checks and the retiring behaviour, skipping only an unmade report, never accepting a contradicting value;
              a substitute (adapter-minted Attempt ids, actor attribution) is a failure of R2 and R3, not a limit.
            - R4, R5: oracles anchor reports to suite facts; every case and oracle cites core Spec rules that resolve;
              every expected value is what its cited rule gives for the fixture (I41); none comes from running a
              runtime.
            - R6, R7, R10, R13: every check, a pair (judge, Invariant), has a mutant caught by a violation naming that
              Invariant; null fails every case by a stated failure; falsify's pass conditions; a mutant's edits stay
              inside its copy.
            - R8, R9: verdicts are a function of code, case and seed; every case runs under its own time bound in
              every mode, an overrun is a defect; one verdict per requested case, in machine and human forms, naming
              the failed expectation, the failing runs, the judge and the rule.
            - R11, R12, R14: the old-suite comparison; every core Spec Invariant in scope (ten Specs, including Compile,
              Diagnostics, Codec and StaticCli) is judged by an oracle or a case expectation, or listed uncovered with
              why; every case is a runtime case or a static case, with a run (one per world seed, at least two, for a
              runtime case) as the unit.

            **The check rounds:**

            - Round 1 (11 gaps): no Requirement judged the core Specs at all (R12), case-only kills were uncheckable,
              the time bound contradicted R8, R2's limits were unbounded, mutant containment, seed and step naming, R1's
              JSON clause, an unrecorded I9 violation, T15 could not fail on I18. Repaired in the Spec only.
            - Rounds 2 and 3: R12's scope (static Specs), R3 against R2, no time bound in process, unrecorded and
              misstated violations (Q11, Q13, Q14), R9/I32 for static cases, R5's main clause had no Invariant (I41).
              Each patch opened gaps where runtime-only wording met static cases.
            - Convergence redesign (2026-11-09): after three ISSUES rounds the contract was redesigned once around one
              case model (R14), keeping the old text as prior art. No ids renumbered; new R14, I40 to I44, Q14, T28.
              Round 1 and 2 fixes were carried over (a sidecar confirmed line by line).
            - Round 4: unrecorded `lane-cells#permittedResend` limit (now in Q8), T13, T17 and Q12's link.
            - Round 5 (`0df8e2a9`): the four earlier gaps closed; the rest were weak test plants (below).

            **Sidecar results, summarised** (all Haiku, effort high, read-only; the lead verified each against the
            code): most findings accepted and fixed; the rejections that matter: "policy quotes absent" and the speed
            targets, `nub run verify` and the coverage record as Spec gaps (they are P3 exit criteria, in the Plan
            record); Invariants resting on pending decisions marked provisional (labelled instead); R1 against Q4 (Q4
            defers by meaning); trailing spaces, continuation indents and the `VERDICTS` line format (formatter output
            and what the parent process parses); `three-channels` line 143 as a violation (`reasonRight`: the rule 
            leaves
            the reason open, not a limit; the `failureKept` branch at :134 stays Q8's because it applies to every
            runtime, production included).

            **Finding:** R7 does not hold today: `suite --runtime null` gives 0/61, but 29 of those are a thrown
            `NoHeldRequest`, which `dsl/case.ts` calls a scenario bug, never a verdict (Q2). The P3 evidence "null
            0/61" overstates what null proves, and T15's "Exists" half cannot fail on I18 today.

            **Location:** the description says next to the suite; the user ruled Specs live in the workflow folder ("it
            should just be in the workflow repo", 2026-11-09); README work list item 7 points there. `derives_from
            [@task.V3.T4]` is allowed; @plan.Runtime's verification target is added once the user approves that Plan
            edit.
